Scidc - Build Instructions
==========================

Scidc is distributed as an AppImage. No system-wide installation is needed.
The following describes how to build the AppImage from source.

Build dependencies
------------------
Install the required development packages (Debian/Ubuntu/Mint):

    sudo apt-get install \
        build-essential \
        libx11-dev \
        libsm-dev \
        libxcursor-dev \
        libfreetype6-dev \
        libfontconfig1-dev \
        libexpat1-dev \
        zlib1g-dev

No Tcl/Tk packages are listed here on purpose. Scidc requires Tcl/Tk 9.0 and
compiles against its *private* headers, which distribution packages usually do
not ship. Step 1 below builds a suitable Tcl/Tk locally.

Building
--------
1. Build a local Tcl/Tk 9 (required once, used inside the AppImage):

    bash build-tcltk.sh

   This also installs Tcl/Tk's private headers, which src/tk/ needs: the
   forked text, treectrl and Tkhtml widgets compile against Tcl/Tk internals.

2. Configure the build system (required on every fresh clone):

    ./configure

3. Compile the C++ code:

    make

4. Create the AppImage:

    bash build-appimage.sh

   On the first run, AppDir/usr/share/scidc-beta/ is populated
   automatically from the source tree. No sudo is required.

   The resulting file is Scidc-x86_64.AppImage.

   Chess engines (Stockfish, Fairy-Stockfish) are not bundled.
   On first start the application offers to download them automatically.

Running
-------
    chmod +x Scidc-x86_64.AppImage
    ./Scidc-x86_64.AppImage

User data is stored in ~/.scidc-beta/

Development
-----------
To run the program directly from the source tree without building
an AppImage (requires AppDir/usr/share/ to exist from a prior
build-appimage.sh run):

    ./run-scidb.sh

To raise the build counter (the bN in the version JJ.MM.TT bN) in all
version files and rebuild everything:

    bash bump-version.sh

Releasing
---------
release.sh publishes a built AppImage as a release on the project's
Forgejo instance. It works in four steps: create and push the tag,
create the release, upload the AppImage, and then verify - the asset is
downloaded back, its SHA-256 compared against the built file, and the
downloaded copy is started with --version.

    bash release.sh              # version taken from Makefile.version
    bash release.sh --dry-run    # run all checks, publish nothing

Before writing anything it checks that the three version files agree,
that the AppImage exists, and that the API answers. Each step is
skipped if it was already done, so an interrupted run can be repeated;
an existing asset of the same name needs --force to be replaced.

The release text defaults to this build's entry in CHANGELOG.md
(-m TEXT or -n FILE override it). Further options: --stable (not a
prerelease), --draft, --no-run (skip starting the downloaded file).

Two things to watch out for:

  - bump-version.sh already sets the tag when it raises the version.
    Commits made afterwards - the CHANGELOG entry, for instance - are
    then outside the tag, and the release would carry an outdated tree.
    release.sh warns when the tag does not point at HEAD; move the tag
    (git tag -d T && git push origin --delete T) and run it again.

  - Credentials for the API come from $FORGEJO_CRED or ~/.git-credentials.
    The secret is never printed and never passed as a command line
    argument, so it does not show up in ps.
